  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-15, on v0.21.0. His words:\n\n> bug; when switching between milling and turning - the world gizmo is not orienting as a lathe normally is oriented.. Z shouldd be right to left, X Back and forth - Y not visible at all tbh. As there is no Y on lathes in most cases.\n\n## What happens\n\nWhen the user changes from a milling machine to a turning machine, the world gizmo and the view keep the milling orientation (Z up).\n\n## Expected\n\nOn a turning machine the view and the world gizmo use the lathe convention:\n\n- The Z axis (the spindle axis) is horizontal on the screen, right to left.\n- The X axis is the cross-slide direction (back and forth).\n- The gizmo does not show the Y axis when the machine has no Y axis. A lathe or mill-turn machine with a Y axis shows it.\n\nA change back to a milling machine restores the milling orientation.\n\n## For the work\n\n1. Find the positive direction of Z and X on the screen from the machine data (chuck side, turret side). Do not hard-code one hand. State the rule in the PR.\n2. This is a display change only. It must not change the WCS, the toolpath or the posted G-code. A test pins that the posted program of a turning golden is the same.\n3. Do this together with #1993 (the lathe work envelope stands vertical). The two have one cause: the viewport has no lathe orientation.\n\n## Done means\n\n- With a turning machine, the default view shows Z horizontal and the gizmo shows Z and X only (and Y only when the machine has a Y axis).\n- With a milling machine, the view and the gizmo are as before.\n- A structural UI test pins the gizmo axes and the view direction for the two machine kinds.\n",
